POLITICAL DEFINITION OF ÁKRATAS GROUP: radical anarchist separatist feminism (FRAS), from the political line of feminism first:
- feminist: because feminism is the guide for women's struggle for their emancipation from patriarchy;
- radical: because radicalness aimed to change the system from its roots and not reform it, much less dress it up as liberal feminism did;
- anarchist: because it was the proposal to destroy this oppressive social system and build another based on self-determination and autonomy;
- separatist: because it was impossible to achieve women's emancipation without separating from the direct executors of oppression, men, in order to strengthen ties between women and
- of the feminism first line: because homosexuals infiltrated in feminism prioritized lesbianism over feminism, focusing on the search for sexual freedom, therefore diverting lesbianism from its fundamental objective: feminism, the emancipation of all women and the abolition of the patriarchal system

ÁKRATAS DID NOT DEFINE ITSELF AS A "LESBIAN GROUP" BECAUSE: 
a- it understood that feminism was in itself love among women; 
b- lesbianism could be confused with "female homosexuality," which is what they tried to avoid;
c- lesbianism could be reduced to "sex between women," that is, a sex-genital matter invisibilizing the sex-political dimension, and 
d- it could divide lesbians from heterosexual women who were their natural allies

MOST RELEVANT POLITICAL POSTULATES OF RADICAL ANARCHIST SEPARATIST FEMINISM (FRAS):
- Feminism is the theory and lesbianism the practice; in fact, feminism = lesbianism;
- Lesbianism is not "sex" between women, but "love" among women, political love (female homosexuality was sex between women);
- Against the lesbianphobia of liberal heterosexual feminism (FHaLF, anti-Lesbian-feminist heterosexual feminism);
- Demarcation from the homosexual movement as misogynistic, and from gayism, furthermore, as commercial;
- Abolition of the State and all patriarchal social institutions: governments, parliament, parties, school, laws, etc. and… also, of family, marriage, monogamy, intercourse, heterosexuality and femininity compulsory for women;
- Disappearance of phallocracy and androcracy;
- Self-management, self-organization, self-direction and self-determination, autonomy.

COMMEMORATION OF ÁKRATAS AND MARCELA OLAVARRIETA. VIDEO BY ANA BERTHA CAMPA. Documentary video: Ákratas 1976 – 1980. The first Mexican group of lesbian feminists, separatists and anarchists and its presentation at the Club de Periodistas, on January 27, 2018. It consists of 3 parts: 
1. History of Ákratas, with the participation, in narration, of Circe Sandoval, Miriam Ruíz, Alejandra Guzmán and Vivian Olmos; 
2. Presentation by Yan María Yaoyólotl of the history of Ákratas 1976 – 1980, and the analysis of the current situation of the lesbian movement in Mexico, with the participation of speakers Jimena Bedregal and Selene Flores. Includes testimonies from participants in the audience: Kitzia Montiel, Evelyn Osorio and Luisa Velázquez;
3. Discussion with Karina Vergara and Luisa Velázquez about the importance of the event in the current context in which lesbians live. Made with archival material from AHMLFM-YMY, audiovisual records of the Ákratas predecessors of the lesbian feminist movement in Mexico event and interviews.

